On 2008-12-16, Ray Dillinger <bear@sonic.net> wrote:
> David Ploog wrote:
>> I think that a game should challenge its
>> master players, too, but that's a different design principle.
> It's a much more difficult design principle to achieve, particularly
> when the code is publically released and people can make arbitrarily
> precise spoilers.  I agree that it's the ideal, but I'm not holding 
> out for it because bluntly I think it's just plain too hard to do. 

It isn't that hard to do, as long as you keep in mind when designing
that your players are going to make precise spoilers.

The trick isn't to worry about the code -- the trick is to make sure
that precise values aren't displayed to players unless necessary such 
that they can't trivially determine the state of the game, and then
trace through the code to find specific probabilities.

The best example here is the #pray threshold for healing in Nethack.
It's possible to precisely determine exactly the point at which one can
burn prayer to fully heal oneself; this means that in many
circumstances, a player can effectively have double their normal maxHP.
A player who is aware of this and saves his #prayers and tracks them
(another knowable value) can significantly increase their chance of
survival... all because of source diving.

That isn't good.  The obvious solution is to both add some 'vagueness'
to the #pray threshhold, and if possible add some "vagueness" to the
player's HP display.  The only reason to display HP to the player is to
enable pinpoint-precise resource management; but the question then is,
is the game more enjoyable with the thrill of danger that comes with not
being sure of exactly when to pull the trigger on hoped recovery?

_That_ part is the complex issue.  :)

